{"result": {"response": {"id": "resp_0fe3ef4fa84275c30169bb3b8a387c8191940f5cafb315e0c1", "created": 1773878154, "model": "gpt-5.2-2025-12-11", "object": "chat.completion", "system_fingerprint": null, "choices": [{"finish_reason": "stop", "index": 0, "message": {"content": "{\n  \"title\": \"Patient Cancer Records: Imaging Results, Reports, and Copy-Keeping\",\n  \"summary\": \"This community centers on patient-directed recordkeeping in cancer care, specifically the recommended action of getting and keeping copies of key clinical records. The primary entities are imaging results and clinical reports (including cancer reports), which are positioned as essential artifacts for care coordination and treatment planning. Relationships highlight two practical access/organization mechanisms: patients are advised to retain copies of imaging- and cancer-related reports as part of “getting copies of records,” and imaging results can be obtained via online patient portals. Collectively, the network describes an information-management workflow in which imaging outputs and written reports about a specific cancer type are gathered and preserved by the patient to support ongoing clinical decision-making and continuity of care. [Data: Entities (163, 2391, 2394, 3614, 3615); Relationships (577, 4232, 7389, 4230)]\",\n  \"findings\": [\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Patient record-copying is the organizing hub of the community\",\n      \"explanation\": \"The central organizing activity is the recommendation that patients obtain and keep copies of important medical records, explicitly including blood tests, imaging results, and reports. This frames the community as a patient-empowerment and care-coordination workflow rather than an organizational or commercial network. [Data: Entities (2394); Relationships (4230, 4232)]\\n\\nWithin this workflow, “getting copies” is not a passive suggestion: it is described as an action to maintain personal records and support coordination across care settings, implying repeated retrieval, storage, and reuse of documents over time (e.g., during follow-up visits or when engaging new clinicians). [Data: Entities (2394)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Imaging results are a key clinical input and a record patients are advised to retain\",\n      \"explanation\": \"Imaging results are defined as the reported outputs from diagnostic imaging (e.g., CT, MRI, PET/CT, ultrasound, mammogram, bone scan) interpreted by a radiologist and communicated to the care team to guide next steps in care and treatment planning. In cancer care, these results may contribute to clinical staging and TNM scoring, and unclear findings may trigger additional studies (with FDG-PET/CT noted as helpful when other imaging is indeterminate). [Data: Entities (163)]\\n\\nThe community’s recordkeeping emphasis extends to imaging: patients are commonly advised to obtain and save imaging results, including both the images and written reports, and to organize them so they can be reviewed over time with providers. This positions imaging artifacts as longitudinal evidence used across multiple clinical decisions, not just single-visit outputs. [Data: Entities (163, 2394)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Online patient portals function as an access channel for imaging results\",\n      \"explanation\": \"A specific relationship ties imaging results to online patient portals, indicating that portals are used to view and download/print imaging results and other records. This identifies a technical mechanism by which patients can operationalize the “get copies” recommendation. [Data: Relationships (577)]\\n\\nBecause the portal linkage is explicitly about viewing and downloading/printing, the network implies a transition from digital access to personal archiving (e.g., saving or printing). This supports the broader theme that patients should maintain readily retrievable copies, even when primary access begins electronically. [Data: Relationships (577); Entities (2394)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Reports are positioned as essential written records spanning pathology/biopsy and imaging summaries\",\n      \"explanation\": \"Reports are described as clinical documents about a patient’s specific cancer type and related diagnostic testing, including pathology and biopsy reports as well as radiology and other imaging reports that summarize clinical findings. Patients are recommended to collect, copy, and organize these documents in a medical binder to keep complete personal records of cancer-related evaluations. [Data: Entities (2391)]\\n\\nThis framing makes “reports” a unifying document category that bridges diagnostic modalities—supporting continuity between imaging interpretations and tissue-based diagnoses. It also aligns directly with the community’s emphasis on patient-managed documentation for longitudinal care. [Data: Entities (2391, 2394)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Keeping copies of reports is explicitly linked to both imaging and the specific cancer type\",\n      \"explanation\": \"A direct relationship connects reports to the action of getting copies of records, specifying that patients are advised to keep copies of reports about imaging and the specific cancer type. This makes report retention a concrete component of the recordkeeping practice described in the community. [Data: Relationships (4232); Entities (2391, 2394)]\\n\\nBy explicitly calling out both imaging-related documentation and the “specific type of cancer,” the network suggests that effective recordkeeping requires preserving not only raw test outputs but also interpretive and diagnostic summaries that define the patient’s condition and guide treatment. [Data: Relationships (4232); Entities (3615, 2391)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Cancer reports are directly about the patient’s specific cancer type\",\n      \"explanation\": \"The community includes “Reports (Cancer Reports)” described as documents d